{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>These are the generations.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah chapter 12): every place where it says אֵלֶּה ('these') and so forth [it nullifies what precedes], and here it nullifies the generations of Esau. But if this was its intent, it was unnecessary, for Esau had already been nullified, and that which proceeds from something nullified is nullified." "<b>Further,</b> one must understand its saying <b>the generations of Jacob — Joseph</b>: and where are the ten tribes? And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 84:6) — some of them said that it intends to say that he is the principal, and some of them said that just as happened to this one, so happened to that one: this one was born circumcised and this one was born circumcised. And this is from the derash." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intent of the verse is along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis 37:2): our father Jacob sought to dwell in tranquility — the turmoil of Joseph leapt upon him. And that is its saying 'these are his generations of Jacob,' meaning: that which befell him from the turmoil, which the verse comes to inform of — the recognition of Joseph and the sorrow of Jacob. It is Jacob who caused it by his seeking to dwell, as it is written 'וַיֵּשֶׁב יַעֲקֹב' ('and Jacob dwelt'), and in this his generations are these: 'Joseph son' and so forth, and the matters rolled on, for a person brings upon himself all that comes upon him. For no evil comes from the good Master to all, and especially to His beloved ones, Jacob and those like him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to inform that even though several evil events passed over Jacob from the day he was — pursued by Esau, by Laban burdened with troubles — all of them are accounted as nothing in comparison to this event of Joseph. And that is its saying <b>אֵלֶּה</b> ('these'): it nullified the events that had passed over him, for all of them are accounted as nothing next to Jacob's feeling of them." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 36b): Joseph was worthy to establish twelve tribes like Jacob, but through the incident of Potiphar, 'וַיָּפֹזּוּ זְרוֹעֵי יָדָיו' ('his arms were made strong') — the ten righteous ones that emerged from his ten fingers were removed, and there remained to him only two — Ephraim and Manasseh. And this is what he alluded to here in saying <b>these are the generations</b> and so forth: it nullified one particular of the twelve who were not in the world for the reason mentioned." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained along the lines of what is said in the book of Psalms (77:16): ״בְּנֵי יַעֲקֹב וְיוֹסֵף סֶלָה״. And they of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 19b) that the reason it traces the sons of Jacob to Jacob and to Joseph — since Joseph sustained them during the two years of famine and provided for them — they were called by his name; thus far. And it is this very thing that he alluded to here by saying <b>these are the generations of Jacob, Joseph</b> — meaning: through the agency of Joseph there was sustenance for all his generations. Or it may intend along this line: the generations of Jacob and also Joseph. And many verses speak thus, as it is written (Exodus 1:2): ״רְאוּבֵן שִׁמְעוֹן״, as though it said ״וְשִׁמְעוֹן״. Here too it is as though it said ״וְיוֹסֵף״, and Scripture gave a reason for calling all of them by his name in that it concluded by saying <b>a lad of seventeen</b> etc., and the events unfolded and he went down to Egypt and rose to greatness, and his brothers needed him and he sustained them and their little ones, and for this reason they are called by his name, for he was the one who nourished and provided for them all." "<b>A lad of seventeen.</b> The reason it was necessary to tell Joseph's age: the intent was to convey what they of blessed memory said in tractate Berakhot (55b), that one should always await a good dream until twenty-two years, etc. And if so, had Scripture not informed us [he was] a lad of seventeen years, we would not have known this." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend along the line of what they said in the Gemara (Kiddushin 29b): ״This one who is superior to my peers, that I married at sixteen,״ etc. — for at sixteen the physical nature and the evil inclination do not overpower a person as they do at seventeen. And this is why Scripture needed to inform us that Joseph was seventeen years old, for through reaching that time there was in him a degree of human valor to contend with his brothers and bring an evil report about them, etc. And see further on." "<b>Was tending</b> etc. It is difficult: it should have said ״he was tending the flock with his brothers,״ for he was not tending his brothers. Further, one needs to understand the intent in saying <b>and he was a lad</b> etc. And our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah, parashah 84) said he behaved in a youthful manner, and according to their words: why [specifically] the sons of Bilhah? And Rashi explained that he was accustomed to the sons of Bilhah — and this is a far-fetched interpretation. Further, one needs to understand his saying <b>his father's wives</b> — who does not know that they were his father's wives? Further, one needs to examine his saying <b>and Joseph brought an evil report of them</b> etc. — what was their evil report, and on what does Scripture rely for us to understand the matter? And Scripture did not come to obscure but to explain." "However, the intent of Scripture is along this line: he was pasturing his brothers in the manner of the flock — meaning, in the manner of the eating of a flock (i.e., eating a limb from a living animal). And his saying <b>and he was a lad</b> means from the time he was a lad he would consort and associate with the sons of Bilhah. And his saying <b>his father's wives</b> means he considered them in the category of his father's wives without any distinction applied to them. And on this reading it is not difficult that it calls him a lad though he was seventeen years old, for it reverts to the earlier time.\n\nAnd from an inference you learn from his saying ״pasturing his brothers in the law of the flock״ — you have learned that they came under suspicion before him on this particular matter. And from his saying ״the sons of Bilhah etc., his father's wives״ — you have learned that it was he who thought thus and no one else.\n\nAnd from now on, when Scripture says ״and Joseph brought an evil report of them to their father,״ it refers to particulars alluded to in what follows. And this is what they of blessed memory said through their holy spirit — that he said of them that they would eat a limb from a living animal, and this is what is alluded to in ״the flock״ (צֹאן). And also their saying that they would demean the sons of the maidservants — this is what is alluded to in his saying ״his father's wives,״ for his thoughts were not their thoughts, and from this it resulted for them to demean them. And perhaps this matter too gave rise to a suspicion of immorality, for when they were considered in their eyes as maidservants and not in the status of wives, Joseph thought that the brothers' intent was in accordance with the Rambam's view (Hilkhot Avadim, chapter 9) regarding one who lies with his maidservant without specification, that the child is a slave unless he specifies explicitly that he lay with her for the sake of marriage. And from now on, when his brothers demean the sons of the maidservants, they revealed their opinion that through unspecified relations the child is a slave, and the maidservants do not have the status of a father's wife but rather like his father's divorced woman and his raped woman who were permitted to them for marriage, and the suspicion is that the men would lie with them, and there is no wrong in their reasoning. But according to Joseph's reasoning, the halakhah would follow the Rif's view (chapter 2 of Yevamot) that one who lies [with a maidservant] without specification has made her a free woman and the child is legitimate, and the law of ״his father's wives״ applies to them. And on this particular matter too, his saying <b>their evil report</b> was intended, and Joseph was punished for the matter — how could he think of the righteous that they would err in this? For even according to the Rambam's view, he would concede regarding a righteous man such as Jacob that he lay with specification, for the sake of marriage. And furthermore, it was stated explicitly (Targum Yonatan ben Uzziel on Genesis 31:4) that when Rachel and Leah gave them [the maidservants] they gave them so that they would be in the presumption of freed women, and through specification the tribes knew the matter." "<b>And the reason</b> that we find the tribes were of one counsel in the sale of Yosef — and included among them were also the sons of the maidservants. Perhaps the prohibition of a limb from a living animal was also specified regarding them. Or it is possible that they showed favoritism to the six sons of the mistress and agreed to their counsel in selling him, even though their heart was whole with him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {9 "The phrase אכילת צאן literally means 'eating of flock'; Or HaChaim reads it as an allusion to the rabbinic tradition that the brothers ate a limb from a living animal (אבר מן החי), which he then states explicitly in the following sentence."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:29.116969828Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}